1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,242
An executive says that, and it sounds
reasonable. The pipeline is the automated run

2
00:00:09,282 --> 00:00:12,757
that checks each change, and
green means it reported success.

3
00:00:13,097 --> 00:00:17,728
After the last five videos you can already
name several ways in which the sentence

4
00:00:17,768 --> 00:00:22,400
is false while every word of the log is
true. The job was skipped by its if, and a

5
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:26,958
skipped job reports success. The failing
command was on the left side of a pipe.

6
00:00:26,998 --> 00:00:30,604
The tests ran on a commit that is
no longer what will be merged.

7
00:00:30,644 --> 00:00:35,048
The run tested the workflow file from
the branch, which the author had edited.

8
00:00:35,388 --> 00:00:40,770
None of these is exotic. Each is a default.
So the way to trust a green result is to

9
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:44,515
be able to defend every line
of the file that produced it.

10
00:00:44,555 --> 00:00:49,808
In this video you read five files that way.
And keep one question open: of all the

11
00:00:49,848 --> 00:00:53,100
lines in such a file, how
many are the test itself?

12
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:58,615
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:58,655 --> 00:01:03,731
A workflow is a file in the repository that
says: when this event happens, run these

14
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:07,628
jobs. A job is a list of steps on
one fresh machine, the runner.

15
00:01:07,668 --> 00:01:10,734
This video is different in
form from the last five.

16
00:01:10,774 --> 00:01:15,789
There's no new concept. There are five
workflow files, and you read each of them in

17
00:01:15,829 --> 00:01:20,296
the fixed order: trigger, permissions,
job and runner, checkout, commands.

18
00:01:20,636 --> 00:01:24,118
The files are in the workflows
directory of the course.

19
00:01:24,158 --> 00:01:29,113
Each carries a header comment, declares
permissions at the top, and pins every

20
00:01:29,153 --> 00:01:32,827
action to a full commit ID,
with the version as a comment.

21
00:01:32,867 --> 00:01:38,207
An action is a packaged, reusable step, and
the permissions say what the job's token

22
00:01:38,247 --> 00:01:41,729
may do. The IDs are listed
in workflows/ACTION_PINS.md.

23
00:01:41,769 --> 00:01:46,852
When I name a version in this video, I'm
reading it from the file on screen, not

24
00:01:46,892 --> 00:01:48,837
from memory, and so should you.

25
00:01:49,177 --> 00:01:54,031
And once more what was verified. These
files were parse-checked and assembled from

26
00:01:54,071 --> 00:01:57,613
documented syntax. The author
didn't execute them on GitHub.

27
00:01:57,653 --> 00:02:02,387
For workflow 5 there is an additional
statement: the Maven module and the plugin

28
00:02:02,427 --> 00:02:05,491
versions in its pom.xml
weren't built by the author.

29
00:02:05,831 --> 00:02:11,020
The versions were confirmed to exist in
Maven Central on the second of October 2026.

30
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:16,250
So if one of these files fails on your
practice repository for a reason nobody could

31
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:21,293
test, you have a real diagnosis exercise,
and the method for it is the subject of

32
00:02:21,333 --> 00:02:22,289
the next module.

33
00:02:22,629 --> 00:02:26,343
The labs of Module 26 all have the same
five movements, and the lab manual names

34
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:30,191
them: add the workflow file, predict when
it runs and what is checked out, run it,

35
00:02:30,231 --> 00:02:32,631
read the log, then break it
on purpose and diagnose.

36
00:02:32,671 --> 00:02:36,245
The manual adds a sentence I'll repeat:
a prediction you didn't write isn't a

37
00:02:36,285 --> 00:02:36,761
prediction.

38
00:02:38,601 --> 00:02:40,179
Here's what you'll walk away with.

39
00:02:40,519 --> 00:02:41,932
After this video you can:

40
00:02:42,272 --> 00:02:45,170
explain every line of the
first five workflows;

41
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:50,036
say for each which event starts it, what
is checked out and which permissions the

42
00:02:50,076 --> 00:02:50,600
token has;

43
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:54,323
predict the outcome of each before
running it and compare with the run;

44
00:02:54,663 --> 00:02:57,384
break each on purpose and
