46
00:03:03,047 --> 00:03:08,111
Workflow 1: run the tests. You read this
file in video 142, so only the summary.

47
00:03:08,151 --> 00:03:13,342
Trigger: pushes to main, and pull requests,
GitHub's proposals to merge one branch

48
00:03:13,382 --> 00:03:18,637
into another. The branch filter on push
stops a second run for every push to a pull

49
00:03:18,677 --> 00:03:23,741
request branch. Permissions: contents:
read, and every other permission is none.

50
00:03:24,081 --> 00:03:29,824
One job on ubuntu-24.04 with a ten-minute
limit. A fixed label doesn't move when

51
00:03:29,864 --> 00:03:35,535
ubuntu-latest migrates. Checkout with the
defaults you know from video 145, one

52
00:03:35,575 --> 00:03:39,222
commit and no tags, and
persist-credentials: false.

53
00:03:39,562 --> 00:03:42,800
A step that prints what was
checked out. Python 3.13, quoted.

54
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:47,261
The tests, with PYTHONPATH set for that
step only, because env at step level is the

55
00:03:47,301 --> 00:03:48,121
narrowest scope.

56
00:03:48,461 --> 00:03:53,694
Quick quiz. On a pull request, which
ref does that printing step show?

57
00:03:53,734 --> 00:03:58,289
A, refs/heads/main. B, the name
of the pull request's branch.

58
00:03:58,329 --> 00:04:00,850
C, refs/pull/N/merge. Your answer?

59
00:04:05,690 --> 00:04:09,275
C, the test merge that GitHub
computes for the pull request.

60
00:04:09,315 --> 00:04:14,231
The textbook's prediction for the printing
step, from the documentation: on a push

61
00:04:14,271 --> 00:04:19,307
to main it prints refs/heads/main, the pushed
commit, true for shallow, and a status

62
00:04:19,347 --> 00:04:24,202
line that names a branch. On a pull request
it prints refs/pull/N/merge, the test

63
00:04:24,242 --> 00:04:28,553
merge commit, true, and "HEAD (no
branch)". Lab 26.1 has you confirm it.

64
00:04:28,893 --> 00:04:33,898
Workflow 2: linting. Linting is an automated
check for mistakes and style, such as

65
00:04:33,938 --> 00:04:39,005
an unused import. Same trigger, same
permissions. The differences are in the steps.

66
00:04:39,345 --> 00:04:44,483
astral-sh/setup-uv installs uv, the tool
these workflows use to provide Python and

67
00:04:44,523 --> 00:04:49,723
install packages. Its python-version input
sets a variable for the rest of the job,

68
00:04:49,763 --> 00:04:53,638
and uv then provides that
interpreter when a command needs it.

69
00:04:53,978 --> 00:04:59,459
uv sync --locked creates the virtual
environment from uv.lock, the lock file that

70
00:04:59,499 --> 00:05:04,981
pins each dependency to an exact version.
It fails if the lock file doesn't match

71
00:05:05,021 --> 00:05:10,638
pyproject.toml. The textbook's summary of
that line: CI installs what was reviewed,

72
00:05:10,678 --> 00:05:11,252
or stops.

73
00:05:11,592 --> 00:05:16,726
Then two checks with IDs: uv run ruff
check, and uv run ruff format --check.

74
00:05:16,766 --> 00:05:20,675
The second has if: !cancelled()
in the expression wrapper.

75
00:05:20,715 --> 00:05:26,122
That replaces the implicit success(), so
formatting is checked even when linting

76
00:05:26,162 --> 00:05:31,637
failed. One push reports both problems.
And the job still fails, because a failed

77
00:05:31,677 --> 00:05:35,109
step fails the job unless
continue-on-error is set.

78
00:05:35,449 --> 00:05:40,326
The last step reads the two step outcomes
through env and appends a Markdown table

79
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:45,062
to the step summary. The braces group the
echo commands so that one redirection

80
00:05:45,102 --> 00:05:46,201
covers all of them.

81
00:05:46,541 --> 00:05:51,745
Workflow 3: build. You read this in video
144. Trigger: pushes to main, pushes of

82
00:05:51,785 --> 00:05:57,054
tags that match v*, and manual runs. The
pattern is quoted, because a star is YAML

83
00:05:57,094 --> 00:06:02,427
syntax at the start of a scalar. branches
and tags under one push are alternatives:

84
00:06:02,467 --> 00:06:06,247
a push matches if it's a matching
branch or a matching tag.

85
00:06:06,287 --> 00:06:11,491
There is no pull_request trigger, so a
pull request that adds this file starts no

86
00:06:11,531 --> 00:06:12,138
run of it.

87
00:06:12,478 --> 00:06:16,207
defaults.run.shell: bash,
so pipelines fail properly.

88
00:06:16,247 --> 00:06:21,968
fetch-depth: 0, because the next step runs
git describe --tags --always and needs

89
00:06:22,008 --> 00:06:27,445
history and tags. That step has the ID
describe and appends to GITHUB_OUTPUT.

90
00:06:27,485 --> 00:06:32,779
The job publishes two outputs. The
second job, report, has needs: build, no

91
00:06:32,819 --> 00:06:38,326
checkout, and reads the outputs through
env. The textbook's sentence: the only

92
00:06:38,366 --> 00:06:42,238
things that reached the second
machine are two strings.

93
00:06:42,578 --> 00:06:45,491
Workflow 4: Python tests
with uv and caching.

94
00:06:45,831 --> 00:06:50,723
Same trigger and permissions as workflows
1 and 2. Then a block you haven't read

95
00:06:50,763 --> 00:06:51,771
yet: concurrency.

96
00:06:52,111 --> 00:06:57,840
The group is built from the workflow name
and github.ref, with cancel-in-progress:

97
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:03,680
true. One run at a time per workflow and
ref. A new push to the same branch or pull

98
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:08,464
request cancels the run in progress.
For pull requests github.ref is

99
00:07:08,504 --> 00:07:12,474
refs/pull/N/merge, so each
pull request is its own group.

100
00:07:12,514 --> 00:07:15,359
Concurrency has its own video, video 150.

101
00:07:15,699 --> 00:07:20,678
The setup step has an ID and two new inputs.
enable-cache: true turns on the cache

102
00:07:20,718 --> 00:07:25,697
built into the action. cache-dependency-glob:
uv.lock makes the lock file the only

103
00:07:25,737 --> 00:07:30,716
input to the key. So a changed lock file
gives a new key and a miss, and any other

104
00:07:30,756 --> 00:07:34,634
change gives a hit. You computed
exactly that in the last video.

105
00:07:34,974 --> 00:07:40,307
The next step prints the action's cache-hit
output, mapped through env, so the log

106
00:07:40,347 --> 00:07:45,287
states which case occurred. Then uv sync
--locked, then uv run pytest, which

107
00:07:45,327 --> 00:07:48,694
collects the unittest classes
of the sample project.

108
00:07:49,034 --> 00:07:50,485
Workflow 5: Java tests with Maven.

109
00:07:50,825 --> 00:07:52,654
This is the file with a path filter.

110
00:07:52,994 --> 00:07:58,315
Under both push and pull_request, two paths:
everything under java-service, and the

111
00:07:58,355 --> 00:08:03,417
workflow file itself. Listing the workflow
file means that an edit to the CI is

112
00:08:03,457 --> 00:08:04,515
tested by the CI.

113
00:08:04,855 --> 00:08:09,929
And here's the sentence from the textbook
that connects to the hook of video 143:

114
00:08:09,969 --> 00:08:15,231
don't make this workflow a required check,
a check that a rule says must pass before

115
00:08:15,271 --> 00:08:18,956
merging. A workflow that the
filter skips creates no check.

116
00:08:19,296 --> 00:08:23,609
The job sets defaults.run.working-directory
to java-service.

117
00:08:23,649 --> 00:08:28,760
The comment in the file states the
limit: it applies to run steps only.

118
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:34,855
uses steps still resolve paths from the
workspace root, which is why the upload path

119
00:08:34,895 --> 00:08:37,176
further down is written in full.

120
00:08:37,516 --> 00:08:42,798
actions/setup-java installs Temurin 21,
and the distribution input is required.

121
00:08:42,838 --> 00:08:48,188
cache: maven restores the local Maven
repository from a cache keyed on the given

122
00:08:48,228 --> 00:08:48,727
pom.xml.

123
00:08:49,067 --> 00:08:53,466
mvn -B verify runs Maven, the
build tool of the Java module.

124
00:08:53,506 --> 00:08:59,681
-B is Maven's batch mode, with no interactive
prompts and no color codes in the log.

125
00:09:00,021 --> 00:09:04,751
The last step uploads the test reports with
if: failure(), so only when an earlier

126
00:09:04,791 --> 00:09:07,252
step failed, and keeps
them for seven days.

127
00:09:07,592 --> 00:09:12,791
What can go wrong across all five. Section
20A.16 is a table of nine symptoms, and

128
00:09:12,831 --> 00:09:18,157
you can now explain each one from a mechanism:
the merge ref, the shallow clone, the

129
00:09:18,197 --> 00:09:23,523
filter that creates no run, the missing
pipefail, the empty output, the if that is a

130
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:28,761
string, the stale cache, the unquoted
version, and the secret that doesn't reach a

131
00:09:28,801 --> 00:09:29,081
fork.

132
00:09:30,921 --> 00:09:31,894
A picture helps.

133
00:09:32,234 --> 00:09:36,890
Every workflow file has four kinds of
lines, and only one of them is the check.

134
00:09:37,230 --> 00:09:40,674
The trigger decides whether a run
exists and which commit it's about.

135
00:09:41,014 --> 00:09:43,629
The permissions decide what
the job's token may do.

136
00:09:43,969 --> 00:09:48,137
The environment setup decides what is on
the machine: the checkout, the language

137
00:09:48,177 --> 00:09:49,241
toolchain, the cache.

138
00:09:49,581 --> 00:09:56,068
And the commands are the check itself:
python -m unittest, uv run ruff check, uv

139
00:09:56,108 --> 00:09:59,005
build, uv run pytest, mvn -B verify.

140
00:09:59,345 --> 00:10:04,777
Try it now. Thirty seconds. Open
04-python-tests.yml in the workflows directory, or

141
00:10:04,817 --> 00:10:09,590
wait for the diagram, and count the
lines in each of the four categories.

142
00:10:09,630 --> 00:10:11,502
How many lines are the check?

143
00:10:13,842 --> 00:10:18,757
Two. The check is two lines. Everything
else exists to make those two lines mean

144
00:10:18,797 --> 00:10:23,960
something: the right commit, the dependencies
that were reviewed, a token that can't

145
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,863
do harm, a run that stops when a
newer push makes it pointless.

146
00:10:28,203 --> 00:10:32,236
That proportion is the answer to the executive
in the hook, and to the question you

147
00:10:32,276 --> 00:10:34,886
kept open. "Green" is a
statement about the two lines.

148
00:10:34,926 --> 00:10:38,076
Whether it's evidence about the
change depends on all the others.

149
00:10:38,416 --> 00:10:43,618
One rule from section 20A.17 follows from
this picture: don't put the only copy of a

150
00:10:43,658 --> 00:10:48,673
procedure in a workflow. A build that
exists only as YAML steps can't be run on a

151
00:10:48,713 --> 00:10:53,604
laptop or on another CI system. Keep the
commands in the repository and let the

152
00:10:53,644 --> 00:10:58,784
workflow call them. Look at the five files
again with that rule in mind: each check

153
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:03,340
is a command you can type locally. The
lab has you do so before you push.

154
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:09,978
Read it from the top bracket down: trigger,
permissions, environment setup, and at

155
00:11:10,018 --> 00:11:11,276
the bottom, two lines.

156
00:11:13,116 --> 00:11:13,876
Into the lab.

157
00:11:14,216 --> 00:11:15,682
Step 1: add.

158
00:11:16,022 --> 00:11:20,626
git commit is 🟢 SAFE: it adds objects
and moves the current branch forward.

159
00:11:20,666 --> 00:11:25,642
Notice the order: the tests run locally
first, with the same command the workflow

160
00:11:25,682 --> 00:11:25,952
uses.

161
00:11:26,292 --> 00:11:30,725
How many files under .github will git
ls-files list, and what does that tell you

162
00:11:30,765 --> 00:11:33,298
about where a workflow
lives? Say it out loud.

163
00:11:41,138 --> 00:11:46,153
One: .github/workflows/01-tests.yml,
a tracked file among the others.

164
00:11:46,193 --> 00:11:50,183
A workflow lives in the
repository like any other file.

165
00:11:50,223 --> 00:11:55,825
The project also contains a Dockerfile,
a Java module, a pyproject.toml and a

166
00:11:55,865 --> 00:11:59,122
deployment script.
Workflows 3 to 7 use them.

167
00:11:59,462 --> 00:12:04,125
Here the lab's second movement would
follow. Before the push that creates the

168
00:12:04,165 --> 00:12:08,644
repository on GitHub, you write down:
the event, the workflow that starts,

169
00:12:08,684 --> 00:12:12,674
GITHUB_REF, GITHUB_SHA, and how
many commits the runner will have.

170
00:12:12,714 --> 00:12:13,529
Don't skip it.

171
00:12:13,869 --> 00:12:15,450
Step 2: break.

172
00:12:15,790 --> 00:12:21,204
git switch -c is 🟢 SAFE: it adds a ref.
The failure scenario changes one expected

173
00:12:21,244 --> 00:12:23,157
value in a test, on a branch.

174
00:12:23,497 --> 00:12:29,243
Locally: exit status 1, one failure. On
GitHub, pushed as a pull request, the same

175
00:12:29,283 --> 00:12:31,784
command is the last step of the job.

176
00:12:32,124 --> 00:12:37,040
Which step of workflow 1 will be red, and
what will the printing step have shown for

177
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:38,515
the ref? Say it out loud.

178
00:12:43,355 --> 00:12:48,155
The last step is the red one, because
the same command exits with status 1.

179
00:12:48,195 --> 00:12:52,866
And by the documentation, the step
that shows what was checked out prints

180
00:12:52,906 --> 00:12:56,802
refs/pull/N/merge for the ref.
Lab 26.1 has you confirm both.

181
00:12:57,142 --> 00:12:58,895
Step 3: recover.

182
00:12:59,235 --> 00:13:04,261
git revert is 🟡: it adds one commit. The
branch moves, the index and files are

183
00:13:04,301 --> 00:13:06,340
updated, and nothing is removed.

184
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:11,123
A new commit that undoes the bad one.
Three subjects in the log, and the last

185
00:13:11,163 --> 00:13:14,674
command prints nothing: the
branch content equals main again.

186
00:13:15,014 --> 00:13:18,047
As a picture: the break, then
the revert on top of it.

187
00:13:18,087 --> 00:13:21,006
The branch history stays
honest about what happened.

188
00:13:21,346 --> 00:13:31,101
Step 4: the Git side of Lab 26.2. Replay
labs/run ch20a/lab-26-2-lint-branch.

189
00:13:34,441 --> 00:13:39,094
A branch that adds an unused import
on purpose, committed and pushed.

190
00:13:39,134 --> 00:13:44,807
git push is 🟡 CAUTION. The last two commands
show what a pull request would list and

191
00:13:44,847 --> 00:13:48,820
show: one commit, 41794f1, and
two added lines in one file.

192
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:53,989
Predict from workflow 2: which step
fails on this, ruff check or ruff format

193
00:13:54,029 --> 00:13:58,003
--check? And does the other one
still run? Say it out loud.

194
00:14:02,843 --> 00:14:06,830
The other one still runs:
you know that from the if.

195
00:14:06,870 --> 00:14:13,104
And the lab manual records a local run
with ruff 0.15.21: ruff check reported the

196
00:14:13,144 --> 00:14:17,906
unused import with exit status
1, and the format check passed.

197
00:14:17,946 --> 00:14:24,025
So on GitHub, expect the step "Lint" to
fail, and "Check formatting" to run and

198
00:14:24,065 --> 00:14:27,046
pass. Lab 26.2 is where you confirm it.

199
00:14:27,386 --> 00:14:31,750
On your practice repository, in your
normal shell. The interface changes.

200
00:14:31,790 --> 00:14:35,370
The lab text and the linked
documentation are the reference.

201
00:14:35,410 --> 00:14:38,085
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

202
00:14:38,425 --> 00:14:43,557
Lab 26.1. After the push, open the run of
