00:15:08,659 --> 00:15:13,179
Then change the lock file as the lab
describes and find it a third time.

210
00:15:13,219 --> 00:15:18,309
In the run list, look for a run that was
cancelled when you pushed again quickly,

211
00:15:18,349 --> 00:15:20,842
and connect it to the concurrency block.

212
00:15:21,182 --> 00:15:26,484
Lab 26.5. Add workflow 5. First open a pull
request that changes only documentation

213
00:15:26,524 --> 00:15:30,539
and look at its list of checks:
is the Java check there at all?

214
00:15:30,579 --> 00:15:35,624
Then one that touches the Java module.
This is where you may meet a failure the

215
00:15:35,664 --> 00:15:39,936
authors couldn't test, since the
Maven module wasn't built by them.

216
00:15:39,976 --> 00:15:45,214
If so, read the log from the checked-out
commit downward, and treat it as practice

217
00:15:45,254 --> 00:15:46,501
for the next module.

218
00:15:46,841 --> 00:15:52,287
For manual runs in these labs, section
20A.18 labels gh workflow run as 🟡 CAUTION:

219
00:15:52,327 --> 00:15:57,304
it starts a run, which uses minutes and
may deploy if the workflow deploys.

220
00:15:57,344 --> 00:16:00,849
The preview is to read the
workflow file at that ref.

221
00:16:02,689 --> 00:16:04,337
Five mistakes to watch for.

222
00:16:04,677 --> 00:16:07,504
Making the path-filtered
workflow a required check.

223
00:16:07,544 --> 00:16:11,946
Root cause: a workflow that a filter skips
never creates its check, so the pull

224
00:16:11,986 --> 00:16:14,532
request waits for a result
that will not come.

225
00:16:14,872 --> 00:16:19,412
Installing without --locked in CI. Root
cause: without the lock file as the

226
00:16:19,452 --> 00:16:24,235
authority, a stale cache or a new upstream
release can change what is installed

227
00:16:24,275 --> 00:16:26,128
between the review and the run.

228
00:16:26,468 --> 00:16:31,881
Adding fetch-depth: 0 to every job. Root
cause: on a repository with a long history

229
00:16:31,921 --> 00:16:37,334
it turns a one-second fetch into a full
clone for every job; only the job that asks

230
00:16:37,374 --> 00:16:39,173
history a question needs it.

231
00:16:39,513 --> 00:16:44,127
Using continue-on-error or if: always()
without a comment and without testing

232
00:16:44,167 --> 00:16:47,149
results. Root cause: both
hide failures by design.

233
00:16:47,489 --> 00:16:52,325
Expecting defaults.run.working-directory
to apply to an action's paths.

234
00:16:52,365 --> 00:16:57,476
Root cause: it applies to run steps
only; uses steps resolve paths from the

235
00:16:57,516 --> 00:16:58,506
workspace root.

236
00:17:00,346 --> 00:17:05,392
Now, out of the lab. A backend team with
a Python service and a small Java module

237
00:17:05,432 --> 00:17:10,541
adopts these five files. Two weeks later
a pull request that changes only a README

238
00:17:10,581 --> 00:17:15,376
can't be merged. The Java workflow had
been added to the required checks "for

239
00:17:15,416 --> 00:17:16,193
consistency".

240
00:17:16,533 --> 00:17:19,911
The diagnosis is the one you
can now do in your head.

241
00:17:19,951 --> 00:17:24,813
Trigger of workflow 5: a path filter.
Changed paths of the pull request: one

242
00:17:24,853 --> 00:17:29,780
Markdown file. No run exists, so no check
is reported, and the ruleset waits.

243
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:35,327
Section 20A.16 gives the fix and the
prevention. The fix: remove the filter from

244
00:17:35,367 --> 00:17:38,869
required workflows, or require
a job that always runs.

245
00:17:38,909 --> 00:17:43,920
The prevention: require one aggregate
job, and filter inside jobs, not on the

246
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:44,510
workflow.

247
00:17:44,850 --> 00:17:50,374
And the last line of section 20A.17 is
the governance point for the whole set: a

248
00:17:50,414 --> 00:17:55,799
workflow file is code that runs with a
token. Anyone who can push a branch can

249
00:17:55,839 --> 00:18:00,111
change what push and pull_request
workflows do on that branch.

250
00:18:00,151 --> 00:18:05,535
Protect .github/workflows/ with CODEOWNERS,
the file that assigns reviewers to

251
00:18:05,575 --> 00:18:10,612
paths, and a ruleset, a named list of
rules, as you learned in video 136.

252
00:18:12,452 --> 00:18:18,098
Your turn. Do Lab 26.5, "Workflow
5, Java tests with Maven", in

253
00:18:18,138 --> 00:18:23,604
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md,
in your normal shell.

254
00:18:23,944 --> 00:18:29,121
Before each push, predict with a three-dot
diff whether the workflow will start, as

255
00:18:29,161 --> 00:18:34,212
you did in video 143. Write the prediction
down, then look at the list of checks.

256
00:18:34,552 --> 00:18:41,073
The challenge is Exercise 26.3, "Six
things the author did not mean", in

257
00:18:41,113 --> 00:18:44,536
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

258
00:18:48,376 --> 00:18:53,158
Answer out loud. A strong answer is in
plain language, because the listener is an

259
00:18:53,198 --> 00:18:57,742
executive, and still names distinct
mechanisms, not one mechanism five times.

260
00:18:57,782 --> 00:19:01,612
Group them by the four brackets.
Did a run exist for this commit?

261
00:19:01,652 --> 00:19:06,434
Was it the commit that will be merged? Did
the step's status reflect the command?

262
00:19:06,774 --> 00:19:11,514
Was the workflow file itself the reviewed
one? Then give controls at organization

263
00:19:11,554 --> 00:19:16,352
level, each matched to a mechanism. The
follow-up asks which of those controls one

264
00:19:16,392 --> 00:19:21,131
engineer with push access to a branch can
undo for their own pull request without

265
00:19:21,171 --> 00:19:25,203
touching any setting. Think about
which file is read from the branch.

266
00:19:27,043 --> 00:19:27,970
Let's land this.

267
00:19:28,310 --> 00:19:29,970
You should now be able to say:

268
00:19:30,310 --> 00:19:34,656
Each of the five files answers four
questions in order: when it runs, with what

269
00:19:34,696 --> 00:19:38,321
token, on what machine and commit,
and which command is the check.

270
00:19:38,661 --> 00:19:43,892
CI installs from the lock file with
--locked, so what runs is what was reviewed.

271
00:19:44,232 --> 00:19:48,936
A cache keyed on the lock file hits on
every change except a dependency change.

272
00:19:49,276 --> 00:19:54,432
A path-filtered workflow must not be a
required check; require one job that always

273
00:19:54,472 --> 00:19:54,749
runs.

274
00:19:55,089 --> 00:19:59,789
These files were parse-checked and assembled
from documentation, not run on GitHub

275
00:19:59,829 --> 00:20:03,662
by the author; the Maven module was
not built by the author either.

276
00:20:05,502 --> 00:20:11,456
Read the first five workflows of
section 20A.13 in Chapter 20A.

277
00:20:11,496 --> 00:20:19,258
